<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Accuracy (%), response lengths (Len), and AES scores on AIME, GPQA, MATH500, and GSM8K for both 7B and 1.5B models across different baselines. We highlight the <span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#C8DCF0;">best results and the <span id="S5.T1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EBF0FC;">second-best results.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.6 Segments or Tokens? ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Beyond Token Length: Step Pruner for Efficient and Accurate Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> summarizes the performance of SP and seven recent baselines across four benchmarks, for both 7B and 1.5B model sizes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall Performance.</span>
SP consistently achieves the best or highly competitive accuracy across all benchmarks and model sizes, while substantially reducing response length compared to most baselines. For 7B models, SP achieves the highest accuracy on MATH500 and GSM8K, and remains competitive on GPQA and AIME24. For 1.5B models, SP outperforms all baselines on GPQA: Diamond, MATH500, and GSM8K, and remains highly competitive on AIME24, demonstrating strong performance even at smaller scales. Experimental results demonstrate that SP effectively preserves model performance while reducing response length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency.</span>
SP generates significantly shorter responses than the R1-Qwen baselines at both model sizes. For example, on AIME24 with the 7B model, SP reduces the average output length by 70% (from 14,839 to 4,502 tokens), and by 67% on MATH500 (from 4,053 to 1,353 tokens), while maintaining or improving accuracy. Similar reductions are observed for the 1.5B model. Compared to RL-based baselines (e.g., O1-Pruner, ShorterBetter), SP achieves similar or higher accuracy with only moderately longer outputs, offering a better trade-off between brevity and correctness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy-Efficiency Tradeoff.</span>
The AES metric quantifies the trade-off between correctness and conciseness. SP achieves the highest AES on most benchmarks for both model sizes, indicating superior balance. Notably, while some baselines (e.g., O1-Pruner, Model Merge) yield shorter outputs, their accuracy drops substantially, resulting in lower AES. In contrast, prompt-based and SFT-based methods (e.g., CCoT, CoT-Valve) achieve moderate brevity but do not match SP in overall performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Different Segmentations</h3>

<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03805v1/fig/trend.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/147;" width="476" height="147" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Comparison of different segmentation methods during training: output length (left) and accuracy change (right) on MATH500. The paragraph-based segmentation achieved the best trade-off between accuracy and output length.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to basic paragraph-level segmentation, we explore three finer-grained approaches: (1) sentence-level segmentation using NLTK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bird (2006)</a></cite>, (2) conjunction-based segmentation (e.g., "wait", "alternative"; full list in Appendix), and (3) paragraph merging based on embedding similarity, using <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">all-MiniLM-L6-v2</span> with a cosine similarity threshold of 0.5.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2 Different Segmentations ‣ 5 Experimenta Result ‣ Beyond Token Length: Step Pruner for Efficient and Accurate Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, segmentation based on paragraph similarity results in the smallest output length reduction, stabilizing around 2,500 tokens. Token-level segmentation achieves the greatest reduction (below 1,000 tokens) but significantly impacts accuracy. Paragraph-based segmentation strikes a balance, moderately reducing length while preserving higher accuracy.
Splitting by paragraph similarity or conjunctions yields fewer steps than standard paragraph segmentation, while sentence-level segmentation produces the most. In summary, finer-grained segmentation reduces length more effectively but typically lowers accuracy, whereas coarser segmentation better preserves accuracy with less reduction in length. Paragraph-based segmentation offers the best balance between accuracy and length.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Ablation Studies</h3>

