<tr id="S5.T2.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">Len.</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">348</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">1134</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">1139</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">1224</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GSM8K</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">Acc.</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">22.5</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">85.8</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">86.5</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">84.2</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">Len.</th>
<td id="S5.T2.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">105</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">319</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">344</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:-0.5pt 3.0pt;">361</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Ablation study on mathematical tasks. "-" denotes removal of the component. <span id="S5.T2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CR</span>: Correct Reward; <span id="S5.T2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">COS</span>: Correct Optimal Step; <span id="S5.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">WRM</span>: Mask Wrong Response; <span id="S5.T2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SAW</span>: Skip All Wrong.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.3 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experimenta Result ‣ Beyond Token Length: Step Pruner for Efficient and Accurate Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents the results of ablation studies conducted on our RL framework across three mathematical datasets. Removing the correct reward component (-CR) leads to a significant drop in accuracy on all datasets, underscoring the crucial role of explicit correctness signals in guiding effective policy learning. The -COS setting, which prioritizes the shortest answer regardless of correctness, results in a notable decrease in accuracy, emphasizing the need to balance optimal step lengths with the correctness of the responses. In the absence of the mask for wrong answers (-WRM), the model is encouraged to produce shorter but incorrect outputs, as evidenced by both the reduced response lengths and lower accuracy. Lastly, removing the skip-all-wrong mechanism (-SAW) further degrades performance, indicating that excluding uninformative, incorrect responses is vital for maintaining stable learning. These findings collectively highlight the importance of each component in enhancing both the accuracy and efficiency of the learned policy. The synergy among these elements is essential for achieving a balanced trade-off between correctness and conciseness in reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Reasoning Analysis</h3>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.03805v1/fig/bar.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/295;" width="476" height="295" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>The Semantic Analysis of Reasoning in R1-Qwen-7B and SP-7B.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we analyze how SP reshapes the stylistic profile of model-generated reasonings. We prompted Gemini 2.5 to label each sentence in the R1-Qwen-7B and SP-7B outputs on the MATH500 and AIME24 according to five reasoning categories:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">➢</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pivotal Reasoning:</span> Core steps that directly advance the solution.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">➢</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Productive Elaboration &amp; Calculation:</span> Supporting explanations or detailed arithmetic.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">➢</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exploring Alternatives:</span> Consideration of different methods or perspectives.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">➢</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verification &amp; Self-Correction:</span> In-line checks or corrections of prior steps.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">➢</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non-Substantive Statements:</span> Remarks that do not contribute to the logical solution path.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.4 Reasoning Analysis ‣ 5 Experimenta Result ‣ Beyond Token Length: Step Pruner for Efficient and Accurate Reasoning in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> plots the resulting category frequencies. Compared to R1-Qwen-7B, SP-7B shows a notable increase in both Productive Elaboration &amp; Calculation and Pivotal Reasoning, with the former rising to 31.33% and the latter reaching 30.33%. This shift highlights a stronger focus on detailed computations and essential logical steps. Meanwhile, the proportion of Exploring Alternatives has decreased significantly, dropping to 6.7%, suggesting that SP-7B is less prone to exploring tangential approaches.
The share of Verification &amp; Self-Correction has also reduced, down to 21.0%, which could indicate a greater level of confidence or fewer instances of error checking. Interestingly, the proportion of Non-Substantive Statements remains largely unchanged, at 10.73%, implying that SP-7B maintains a similar level of filler content compared to R1-Qwen-7B.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, these shifts suggest that SP-7B drives the model toward more concentrated, substantive, and goal-oriented reasoning, with a notable reduction in digressions and error correction.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces Step Pruner (SP), a reinforcement learning framework that enhances the efficiency of LRMs by reducing unnecessary reasoning steps. Unlike methods that directly penalize output length, SP employs a step-based reward mechanism to ensure concise and accurate answers. Experiments on four benchmark tasks show that SP significantly shortens output length while maintaining logical coherence and achieving state-of-the-art accuracy. Further analysis confirms the effectiveness of the step-based reward and each reward component. SP thus provides a simple and robust solution for efficient reasoning in LRMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While Step Pruner effectively reduces redundant reasoning steps and overall response length, it may inadvertently encourage the model to merge distinct logical steps into overly long paragraphs, potentially compromising interpretability and readability. Additionally, the reliance on paragraph-based segmentation may not capture fine-grained reasoning boundaries in all cases, especially for tasks requiring nuanced step differentiation. Finally, SP’s effectiveness is contingent on accurate evaluation of correctness; in domains with ambiguous or subjective answers, reward assignment may be less reliable, limiting generalizability.</p>
</div>
</section>
